  "body": "There's an undocumented quirk regarding percentage block-size replaced elements inside table cells. This quirk is implemented by EdgeHTML, Chromium, and WebKit (but not Gecko, it seems, at least not with the test I'm presenting here):\r\n\r\n```\r\n<!-- quirks mode -->\r\n<style>\r\n  .cell {\r\n    display: table-cell;\r\n    width: 400px;\r\n    height: 400px;\r\n    background: red;\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  .descendant {\r\n    display: block;\r\n    margin: 0;\r\n    border: none;\r\n    padding: 0;\r\n    width: 100%;\r\n    height: 100%;\r\n    background: green;\r\n  }\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n<p>Green square below?</p>\r\n<div class=\"cell\">\r\n  <div>\r\n    <iframe class=\"descendant\"></iframe>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe percentage height specified on .cell is used to resolve the height of .descendant. This doesn't happen if .descendant is a DIV instead of an IFRAME.\r\n\r\nThis quirk is not documented at https://quirks.spec.whatwg.org/",
